# GitHub Workflow (compulsory)

All work follows this sequence. Scope: all work.

## 1. Issue first

Create a GitHub issue using the **Bounded Context Work Item** before any code changes. If the user requests work and no issue exists, create the issue first—never start coding without an issue.

**Body structure (API-created issues):**
```
**Type:** feat | fix | chore | docs
**Bounded context:** web | cms | ai-orchestrator | contracts | infra | mobile-ios | mobile-android | tooling (if outside project work)
**Expected outcome:** [clear, testable outcome]
**Impacted folders:**
- apps/web
- packages/contracts
**Contract impact:** [ ] Contracts will change  [x] Contracts unchanged
```

- Title format: `type(scope): description` (e.g. `feat(web): add validation`, `chore(tooling): add commitlint`)
- Label: `scoped`

## 2. Branch

Checkout/create branch from `main` using `fix/123-slug` or `feat/123-slug` (issue number + short slug).

## 3. Work

Make changes within the bounded context of the issue. When contracts change: run codegen in same PR and tick "Regeneration Required: yes" in PR template.

## 4. Commits

Produce a series of commits—one per small block of work. Each commit must use conventional format (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:` etc.). Atomic and reviewable.

## 5. PR

Rebase on `main`, then open PR targeting `main`. Use same title format as issue: `type(scope): description`. Fill PR template (Summary, Contracts Changed, Regeneration Required, Validation). Include `Resolves #123` in description.

## 6. Checks

Ensure all CI checks pass before marking work complete. Use `mcp_GitHub_pull_request_read` with `method: get_status` to verify. Re-run or fix failures.
